{
  "id": "a1-09",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 11,
  "title": "The Locative and Place",
  "summary": "Where a thing is and where it is going: the locative, which needs no preposition at all, against *į* with the accusative for direction, and the place prepositions with the cases they govern.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "locative-singular",
      "number": "59",
      "heading": "The Locative Singular",
      "statement": "The **locative** answers *kur?* (where?) and means *in* or *at*. It is remarkable for needing **no preposition**: the ending alone carries the sense that English must express with a separate word. *Vilniuje* is \"in Vilnius\" entire.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Nominative → locative singular",
          "columns": [
            "nominative",
            "locative",
            "example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "masc. -as",
              "cells": [
                "-as",
                "-e",
                "miestas → mieste"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -is",
              "cells": [
                "-is",
                "-yje",
                "brolis → brolyje"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -ys",
              "cells": [
                "-ys",
                "-yje",
                "obuolys → obuolyje"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -us",
              "cells": [
                "-us",
                "-uje",
                "sūnus → sūnuje"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -a",
              "cells": [
                "-a",
                "-oje",
                "knyga → knygoje"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -ė",
              "cells": [
                "-ė",
                "-ėje",
                "katė → katėje"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš gyvenu Vilniuje.",
          "gloss": "I live in Vilnius.",
          "note": "Vilnius is a -us noun"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ji dirba universitete.",
          "gloss": "She works at the university."
        },
        {
          "target": "Knyga yra kavinėje.",
          "gloss": "The book is in the café."
        }
      ],
      "footnotes": [
        "\"At home\" is *namie*, an old form standing outside the paradigm; *name* would be understood as \"in the building\"."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "noun-endings",
        "genitive-endings"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "locative-plural",
      "number": "60",
      "heading": "The Locative Plural",
      "statement": "The locative plural is built on the plural stem and ends in **-uose**, **-ose** or **-ėse**. It too takes no preposition.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Locative plural",
          "columns": [
            "nominative",
            "locative pl.",
            "meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "masc. -as",
              "cells": [
                "namas",
                "namuose",
                "in the houses"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "masc. -is",
              "cells": [
                "brolis",
                "broliuose",
                "in the brothers"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -a",
              "cells": [
                "knyga",
                "knygose",
                "in the books"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "fem. -ė",
              "cells": [
                "katė",
                "katėse",
                "in the cats"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Vaikai yra namuose.",
          "gloss": "The children are at home.",
          "note": "the plural is the ordinary way of saying 'at home' of several people"
        },
        {
          "target": "Ką tu skaitai knygose?",
          "gloss": "What do you read in books?"
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "locative-singular",
        "plural-nominative"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "direction-i",
      "number": "61",
      "heading": "Direction with Į",
      "statement": "Where the locative says where a thing **is**, the preposition **į** with the **accusative** says where it is **going**. The pair is exact, and the case is what carries the difference: *mieste*, in the town; *į miestą*, into the town. Verbs of motion — *eiti*, to go on foot; *važiuoti*, to go by vehicle — take it.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Rest against motion",
          "columns": [
            "where? (locative)",
            "where to? (į + accusative)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "town",
              "cells": [
                "mieste — in town",
                "į miestą — into town"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "university",
              "cells": [
                "universitete",
                "į universitetą"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "café",
              "cells": [
                "kavinėje",
                "į kavinę"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "Lithuania",
              "cells": [
                "Lietuvoje",
                "į Lietuvą"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš einu į kavinę.",
          "gloss": "I am going to the café."
        },
        {
          "target": "Aš esu kavinėje.",
          "gloss": "I am in the café."
        },
        {
          "target": "Jie važiuoja į Vilnių.",
          "gloss": "They are travelling to Vilnius."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "locative-singular",
        "accusative-nouns"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "place-prepositions",
      "number": "62",
      "heading": "The Place Prepositions",
      "statement": "Prepositions of place govern the **genitive**, with the single exception of *į*. *Ant* (on), *prie* (by, near), *už* (behind), *po* (under, in the sense of place, with the instrumental) and *iš* (out of) were begun in ¶33; set beside the locative they complete the ways of saying where a thing is.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "Saying where",
          "columns": [
            "form",
            "case",
            "meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "mieste",
              "cells": [
                "locative, no preposition",
                "in the town"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "ant stalo",
              "cells": [
                "ant + genitive",
                "on the table"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "prie lango",
              "cells": [
                "prie + genitive",
                "by the window"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "už namo",
              "cells": [
                "už + genitive",
                "behind the house"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "į miestą",
              "cells": [
                "į + accusative",
                "into the town"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "*po* meaning \"under\" takes the instrumental — *po stalu*, under the table — and is left until that case is taken up. *po* with the genitive means \"after\" and is temporal (¶33)."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Katė yra po stalu, o knyga ant stalo.",
          "gloss": "The cat is under the table, and the book on it."
        },
        {
          "target": "Mes stovime prie namo.",
          "gloss": "We are standing by the house."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "genitive-prepositions",
        "locative-singular",
        "direction-i"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "place-adverbs",
      "number": "63",
      "heading": "The Place Adverbs",
      "statement": "Three adverbs answer *kur?* without any noun: **čia** (here), **ten** (there), **namie** (at home). Like all adverbs they never change. *Kur* itself asks the question, and a place answer stands in whatever form the sense requires — a locative, or a preposition with its case.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Kur tu esi? — Čia.",
          "gloss": "Where are you? — Here."
        },
        {
          "target": "Jis yra namie.",
          "gloss": "He is at home."
        },
        {
          "target": "Ten dirba mano brolis.",
          "gloss": "My brother works there."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "question-adverbs",
        "locative-singular"
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "motion-verbs",
      "number": "64",
      "heading": "Going on Foot and Going by Vehicle",
      "statement": "Lithuanian distinguishes what English does not: **eiti** is to go on foot, **važiuoti** to go by any vehicle. Choosing wrongly is not a small error — *einu į Vilnių* says you propose to walk there. Both take *į* with the accusative for the destination.",
      "paradigms": [
        {
          "caption": "eiti (eina) and važiuoti (važiuoja) — present",
          "columns": [
            "eiti — to go on foot",
            "važiuoti — to go by vehicle"
          ],
          "rows": [
            {
              "label": "aš",
              "cells": [
                "einu",
                "važiuoju"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "tu",
              "cells": [
                "eini",
                "važiuoji"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "jis, ji",
              "cells": [
                "eina",
                "važiuoja"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "mes",
              "cells": [
                "einame",
                "važiuojame"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "jūs",
              "cells": [
                "einate",
                "važiuojate"
              ]
            },
            {
              "label": "jie, jos",
              "cells": [
                "eina",
                "važiuoja"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "footnote": "Both are of the first conjugation; the -j- of važiuoti belongs to the stem."
        }
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "Aš einu į kavinę.",
          "gloss": "I am walking to the café."
        },
        {
          "target": "Aš važiuoju į Kauną.",
          "gloss": "I am travelling to Kaunas."
        }
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "direction-i",
        "present-conj-i"
      ],
      "core": true
    }
  ],
